          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my father had a heart attack at 55.",
          "answer": "Your father's heart attack at 55 is a risk factor for you. It does not mean you will have one. The guidance I could access calls family history of premature cardiovascular disease a \"risk-enhancing feature.\" It is not a diagnosis, and it is used alongside other factors (Source: *Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*). The documents I retrieved do not give the exact age cut-off that counts as \"premature,\" so ask a clinician how your father's age applies to you.\n\n**Factors that matter most**\n\nGuidance on coronary artery disease lists the contributing factors (Source: *Coronary artery disease*):\n- Smoking\n- High blood pressure\n- Raised LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol\n- Diabetes\n- Excess weight\n- Physical inactivity\n- A diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fiber\n- Chronic kidney disease and some inflammatory conditions\n\nYour age and sex cannot change, but most of the other factors can. Lifestyle guidance supports:\n- **Regular physical activity**, and breaking up long periods of sitting (Source: *Physical activity for high blood pressure*).\n- **More vegetables, fruit, beans and dairy** for potassium, and less salt. Read labels for sodium per serving (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*).\n- **Limiting alcohol.** The commonly stated limit is up to two drinks a day for men, with less being better (Source: *Diet for high blood pressure*).\n\n**When to discuss screening**\n\n- Ask a clinician about your overall cardiovascular risk. Risk is estimated from age, sex, cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes and smoking status, for adults aged 40 to 75 without known heart disease. Family history can shift the decision when the estimate is borderline or intermediate (Source: *Lipids: screening intervals*).\n- Diabetes guidance screens all adults from age 35, so ask about a blood sugar test if you have not had one recently (Source: *Type 2 diabetes: who is screened*).\n- I could not retrieve the guidance on how often to repeat these checks. Ask your clinician.\n\n**Seek urgent care now if you have chest pain or pressure that is severe, spreading, or with breathlessness, sweating or nausea.** Call emergency services rather than driving yourself. Acting early is the safer choice, even if you are unsure (Source: *Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*).\n\nI could not complete a search on screening intervals and family history because the tool budget ran out. This answer is therefore based on the passages above only. Your clinician can review your personal risk.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
